# tools/setup/manifests/one-liner-tools — the one-liner installer REGISTRY (operator 2026-05-31):
# declarative entries for tools/CLIs that DON'T come from a package manager and ship their own
# install SCRIPT. Package-manager CLIs (claude/codex/gemini = bun-global) live in manifests/agent-clis;
# system tools in manifests/{apt,brew,windows}; runtimes in .mise.toml. This file is the escape
# hatch for everything else, kept declarative like the rest.
#
# Installed by common/one-liner-tools.sh (Unix). For each entry the runner DOWNLOADS the install
# script to a temp file (curl_fetch, retry-equipped), VERIFIES it is non-empty, then EXECUTES it
# noninteractively (stdin from /dev/null) — the B-0063 download-then-exec discipline. NEVER curl|bash
# pipe-to-shell: a truncated/interrupted download is caught before any code runs, and a failed
# download surfaces as a non-zero exit (unlike `curl … | bash`, where bash exits 0 on an empty pipe).
# Same shape as the Homebrew bootstrap in macos.sh.
#
# DETECT-FIRST: skip if <detect-binary> is already on PATH (efficient — no re-download; these CLIs
# self-update, or set ZETA_FORCE_UPDATE_TOOLS=1 to force a re-run). NON-INTERACTIVE DEFAULT-SKIP:
# skipped entirely in non-interactive runs (CI gate jobs, Docker build steps, shield steps — no TTY)
# unless ZETA_INSTALL_FULL=1; interactive dev shells run them. BEST-EFFORT: a failed installer WARNS
# and continues — these are auth-gated peer/dev CLIs, not hard deps; LOGIN/auth is the operator's to
# do after install; never bricks install (mirrors common/local-llm.sh exceptions-as-signals).
#
# Format per line:  <detect-binary>  <installer-url>  [key=value ...]
#   key=value qualifiers (optional, order-independent):
#     interp=bash|sh   interpreter the downloaded script is exec'd with        (default: bash)
#     os=mac,linux     restrict to these OSes (uname: Darwin=mac, Linux=linux) (default: all)
#     args=<arg>       extra arg passed to the installer (repeatable: args=--a args=--b)
# `#` comments + blank lines ignored. Unix only; Windows ships its own iex/PS installers + registry.
#
# Pins per dep-pin-search-first (WebSearch + authoritative vendor docs 2026-05-31;
# forge=ForgeCode operator-confirmed 2026-05-31):
#   grok          Grok Build (xAI)            https://x.ai/cli        — install.sh detects OS/arch
#   cursor-agent  Cursor CLI                  https://cursor.com/cli  — install script detects OS/arch
#   kiro-cli      Kiro CLI                    https://kiro.dev/docs/cli/installation
#                 — the curl installer is macOS-ONLY per the docs; Linux uses AppImage/.deb/zip and the
#                   binary is `kiro-cli`. So: os=mac here; Linux auto-install is a best-effort GAP
#                   (Linux users install Kiro manually for now — AppImage flow doesn't fit the
#                   download-script-then-exec shape; revisit if Linux Kiro demand warrants it).
#   hermes        NousResearch hermes-agent   https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent
#                 — bundles a local Hindsight memory daemon on first use (heavy installer: clones repo
#                   + sets up uv/Python). Its setup wizard reads /dev/tty directly (so the /dev/null
#                   stdin redirect alone won't suppress it on an interactive dev install); the only
#                   noninteractive suppressor is the --skip-setup flag (verified against the upstream
#                   installer 2026-05-31), so: args=--skip-setup. Auth/setup is the operator's to run
#                   later (`hermes` / `hermes model`).
#   forge         ForgeCode (operator's forge) https://forgecode.dev  — installer uses sh
grok          https://x.ai/cli/install.sh
cursor-agent  https://cursor.com/install
kiro-cli      https://cli.kiro.dev/install                                                            os=mac
hermes        https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/main/scripts/install.sh    args=--skip-setup
forge         https://forgecode.dev/cli                                                               interp=sh
